{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>The reward of humility is the fear of YHWH, wealth, honor, and life</b> (Proverbs 22:4)." "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, informs us in this verse (Proverbs 22) that with the trait of humility there are bound several good virtues. By way of the plain sense (peshat), the explanation of the verse is that the reward and benefit that accrue from humility in this world are four things: fear, wealth, honor, and life. For the trait of humility is a physical trait in the company of creatures — that a person be shy and patient, honoring others and speaking of their good, and that he hear his disgrace and keep silent. And through this physical trait a person ascends to the trait of fear, which is an intellectual trait, and he will also come to wealth, for the master of humility rejoices in his portion. And it is known that great wealth seems little in a person's eyes together with pride, whereas a little suffices him together with humility. And since he is satisfied with little and does not lust after advantages, and rejoices in his portion — lo, this is wealth, as our Sages of blessed memory expounded: 'Who is wealthy? One who rejoices in his portion.' And he will also come to honor, for when he refrains from lusts and is lowly of spirit, and it suffices him with what has fallen to his portion — lo, this is honor for him, as it is written (Proverbs 29:23): ״ושפל רוח יתמוך כבוד״, and it is written (Proverbs 18:12): ״ולפני כבוד ענוה״ — for whoever has honor, the trait of humility has preceded it already. And he will come to life, for one who lusts after advantages is anxious when he does not grow rich and his strength does not rise according to his soul's desire, and his life is a life of suffering because he did not attain all his desire. And anxiety shortens life; he is found suffering over a world that is not his. But one who rejoices in his portion will not worry over what he has not acquired, and will escape anxiety and live a life of tranquility." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'The reward of humility is the fear of YHWH' — what wisdom made a crown for its head, humility made a heel for its sole, as it is written (Psalms 111:10): ״ראשית חכמה יראת ה׳״, and it is written (Proverbs 22:4): ״עקב ענוה יראת ה׳״. They interpreted the word עקב in the sense of heel, and we learn from this that the trait of humility is greater than wisdom, for the fear that is at the head of wisdom — lo, it is the heel at the feet of humility. And know that every trait among the traits has two extremes, a first extreme and a last extreme, and there is also a third, which is the mean. And the trait of humility is intermediate, for it is the middle between the first extreme, which is pride, and the second extreme, which is self-abasement. Now the mean in all traits is the balanced and good thing for a person to choose for himself and to conduct himself by, and not to incline toward either of the extremes, which is an evil and bitter thing. And of this Solomon the king, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 4:26): ״פלס מעגל רגלך וכל דרכיך יכונו״ — he intended by this to admonish a person to guide himself in the mean between the two extremes, in the manner of the balance-scale (פלס), which is intermediate between the two pans equally and does not deviate outward; and then all his traits will be established, for it is the straight path to walk upon. And this is what he added in explanation and said: 'Do not incline right or left' — meaning that he should walk in the middle and not incline toward either of the extremes. But in the trait of humility we were admonished that a person should incline himself toward the last extreme, which is the trait of self-abasement, and not choose to remain in the middle path, lest he draw near to the boundary of pride, which is the repugnant trait that harms a person and afflicts him in body and soul in the incidental life and in the eternal life. And of this our Sages of blessed memory said in their moral instruction: 'Be very, very lowly of spirit' — and they doubled the word in saying מאד מאד ('very, very') to teach the people understanding that he should incline himself toward the last extreme, which is self-abasement, because the trait of humility is glorious and distinguished, and its virtue is mighty and well-known to the eye. Therefore David the king, peace be upon him, said of himself (Psalms 51:19): ״לב נשבר ונדכה״ — 'a broken and contrite heart' — even though he was a great king and a prophet, and the greatest of the seventy elders, as it is written (II Samuel 23:8): ״יושב בשבת תחכמוני״; he surpassed in the trait of humility and called himself 'a broken and contrite heart.' And so we find with Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, who was the head of all the prophets, that Scripture did not praise him for the other exalted traits that were in him but only for the trait of humility — this is what it says (Numbers 12:3): ״והאיש משה עניו מאד״. And it mentioned מאד ('exceedingly') to explain that Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, did not wish to stand only at the mean, which is the sense of עניו, but inclined himself from the mean toward the side of self-abasement; therefore it mentioned מאד. And included in his humility and lowliness is what we find: that he did not wish to enter the Tent of Meeting while the Tent was still hidden and covered by the cloud of glory, as it is written (Exodus 40:34): ״ויכס הענן את אהל מועד וכבוד ה׳ מלא את המשכן״ — and even though it had already been said to him from the mouth of the Almighty: ״ונועדתי לך שם ודברתי אתך מעל הכפרת מבין שני הכרובים״, he did not assure his heart to enter the Tent of Meeting, neither to prophesy within it nor to pray and offer a sacrifice, until the Holy One, blessed be He, gave him permission and called him. And this is what is written:" "<b>And He called to Moses.</b> The language of calling (קריאה) is a language of distinction — a language that the ministering angels use, as it is stated (Isaiah 6): ״וקרא זה אל זה ואמר״. And we find that through the calling of the Holy One, blessed be He, to a prophet, the truth of three matters is established, and they are the roots of the Torah and the pillar of faith, and these are: the creation of the world, the matter of the giving of the Torah to Israel, and the matter of the resurrection of the dead. And these three matters — the first at the beginning of time, the second in the middle of time, and the third at its end — and each one necessitates the other. For the prophecy of the prophet is clear testimony to divine providence, and providence is a proof of the creation of the world; and since the world is created and providence flows there, it is fitting that the Torah be given in it, through which creatures will merit the resurrection of the dead, which is the end of delight and the ultimate reward at the extremity of time. And in each of these three matters we find the language of calling: the giving of the Torah — this is what Isaiah the prophet mentioned (Isaiah 48): ״שמע אלי יעקב וישראל מקוראי אני הוא אני ראשון אף אני אחרון״ — he mentioned here three modes of being: הוה (is), והיה (was), ויהיה (will be)." "<b>And behold,</b> this verse speaks of the giving of the Torah, and it is known that at that event the Holy One, blessed be He, was proclaimed as first and last and existent in all times, every one. And from this He said: ״ושראל מקוראי״ — for He is the one who calls at Sinai with these two names, יעקב and ישראל, as it is written (Exodus 19:3): ״כה תאמר לבית יעקב ותגיד לבני ישראל״. Behold, the language of calling is mentioned in connection with the giving of the Torah. The creation of the world — this is what is mentioned in the verse after it (Isaiah 48:13): ״אף ידי יסדה ארץ וימיני טפחה שמים קורא אני אליהם יעמדו יחדו״ — to show that all the parts of existence, and they are above and below, are bound one to the other; one cannot exist without the other. The resurrection of the dead — we find in it too the language of calling, this is what David the king, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 50:1): ״אל אלהים ה׳ דבר ויקרא ארץ ממזרח שמש עד מבואו״, and it is written (Psalms 50:4): ״יקרא אל השמים מעל ואל הארץ לדין עמו״, and our Sages of blessed memory expounded: 'יקרא אל השמים מעל — this is the soul; ואל הארץ לדין עמו — this is the body.' And the intention is in order to give judgment and accounting at the time of the resurrection of the dead. From this you learn that the calling of the Holy One, blessed be He, to a prophet includes these three matters, which are the principles of the Torah, and in each and every one of them is the language of calling. Therefore this third book begins with the language of calling: the creation of the world is mentioned in the book of Genesis; the giving of the Torah in the book of Exodus; the resurrection of the dead in the book of Leviticus. Behold, the expression ויקרא includes all three of them." "<b>And know</b> that the verse should properly have said ויקרא ה׳ אל משה וידבר אליו ('And YHWH called to Moses and spoke to him'), and the fact that it says ויקרא אל משה ('and He called to Moses') without specification is to indicate that although the book of Leviticus is a book unto itself, it is all joined and connected to what is written above, for the entire Torah is one structure and one bond. And the word ויקרא refers back to the Glory mentioned above — וכבוד ה׳ מלא את המשכן ('and the glory of YHWH filled the Tabernacle') — and the Glory that calls is the הא of בהבראם, which is small (ze'ira); therefore the aleph of ויקרא is small (ze'ira), and this is clear. And as for what it says מאהל מועד ('from the Tent of Meeting'), our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in Torat Kohanim that the sound would be cut off and not go forth outside the Tent. You might think it was because the sound was low — Scripture says את הקול ('the voice'), and what is 'the voice'? That which is described in the Writings (Psalms 29:4–5): ״קול ה׳ בכח קול ה׳ בהדר קול ה׳ שובר ארזים״. If so, why does it say מאהל מועד? To teach that the sound was cut off — as in (Ezekiel 10:5): ״וקול כנפי הכרובים נשמע עד החצר החיצונה כקול אל שדי בדברו״. You might think it was because the sound was low — Scripture says ״כקול אל שדי בדברו״, and it is written (Ezekiel 1:24): ״ואשמע את קול כנפיהם כקול מים רבים כקול שדי בלכתם קול המולה כקול מחנה״. The explanation of this is that the prophet likened the sound to four things: to many waters for the constancy of the sounds; to the voice of Shaddai for the equivalence of the sounds; to a voice of tumult for the multiplicity of the sounds; to the voice of a camp for the gathering of the movement of the sounds. And this wondrous, resounding sound was confined within the court and was not heard outside it. And the explanation of כקול אל שדי בדברו — that is, in His speaking at Mount Sinai — is from what is written there (Deuteronomy 5:19): ״קול גדול ולא יסף״, and it is written (Song of Songs 5:6): ״נפשי יצאה בדברו״, and it was not heard outside the camp of Israel; so too here, the sound of the wings of the cherubim was not heard outside the court. You will find something analogous in the matter of Samuel the prophet, for at the beginning of his prophecy, when he went to Eli the first time, Eli answered him (I Samuel 3:6): ״לא קראתי בני שוב שכב״ — because Eli thought perhaps no one had called at all and Samuel had erred through the imaginative faculty within him. And when he returned to him a second time, then he knew clearly that he had been called, but he did not know who the caller was — whether it was a man or an angel — therefore he answered him, 'I did not call; lie down again.' And still he could not determine for himself that the caller was an angel. And when Samuel went from before him, Eli's ears were attentive — perhaps he would hear the voice of the caller as Samuel did, and from this he would know that the caller was a human being; and if Eli did not hear while Samuel did, it would become clear to him that the caller was an angel, for such is the nature of prophecy. And when He continued to call Samuel a third time and Eli heard nothing at all, then the matter was confirmed for Eli — that the voice of the caller was an angel; and this is what is written (ibid.): ״ויבן עלי כי ה׳ קורא לנער״. And just as we find this — that it is among the laws of prophecy regarding the operations of hearing — so too do we find regarding the operations of sight, for the Holy One, blessed be He, shows one matter to whom He wills and conceals and hides it from whom He wills, all at the same time and in the same place; as it is written (Daniel 10:7): ״וראיתי אני דניאל לבדי את המראה והאנשים אשר היו עמי לא ראו את המראה״. And so too we find in the signs and wonders that the Holy One, blessed be He, performed in Egypt — that the plague was upon the Egyptians and the deliverance upon Israel, and so the darkness was upon the Egyptians for three days while for all the children of Israel there was light in their dwellings, all in one place and at one time. And this is the language of the Gaon our master Saadiah of blessed memory: 'It is a clear matter that the sound of the speech heard by the prophet is something newly created and an act among the acts of the Blessed Name. Now the prophecy of all other prophets came through the medium of an angel, for the angel would hear the wondrous sound and communicate it to the prophet. But Moses our master, peace be upon him, stood in the place of the angel, for he himself heard the sound of the wondrous speech without intermediary; and this is what is written of him (Deuteronomy 34:10): ״פנים אל פנים״, and it says ״אשר ידעו ה׳״. And this was the superiority of Moses and his advantage over the rest of the prophets, and concerning this the verse testified about him (ibid.): ״ולא קם נביא עוד בישראל כמשה״ — for he was distinguished from them and superior to them in three ways. The first: that his prophecy was without intermediary. The second: in the matter of recovery — for all the other prophets, at the time of prophecy, were terrified and Recoiled and had no response in their mouths, and could not reply until the glory of the Shekhinah departed from them, as is known from Isaiah and Ezekiel; but Moses our master, peace be upon him, was stronger and his level was elevated, and he would reply on the spot himself, as it is written (Exodus 18:12): ״משה ידבר והאלהים יעננו בקול״. And the third: in the matter of the degrees of prophecy — for the other prophets received their prophecy through nine screens, and this is what we find in Ezekiel: nine visions, as it is said (Ezekiel 43:3): ״וכמראה המראה אשר ראיתי כמראה אשר ראיתי בבואי לשחת את העיר ומראות כמראה אשר ראיתי על נהר כבר ואפול על פני״, and so we find in Daniel nine visions, as it is written (Daniel 10:5): ״ואשא את עיני ואראה״ etc., and it is written (ibid.): ״ופניו כמראה ברק״, and it is written (ibid.): ״וראיתי אני דניאל לבדי את המראה והאנשים אשר היו עמי לא ראו את המראה״, ״ואני נשארתי לבדי ואראה את המראה הגדולה הזאת״, and it says beforehand ״ובינה לו במראה״ — behold, nine visions. But Moses our master, peace be upon him, through one screen, through one vision, as it is written (Numbers 12:8): ״ומראה ולא בחידות״. End of his words, of blessed memory." "<b>And now</b> you must reflect that these nine visions are nine specula (mirrors), and the intent is not upon the nine Sefirot, for the prophecy of all the prophets — except for Moses — comes only through the tenth. Therefore these specula are the gradations of this prophecy, one above the other, in the manner of what is said (Ezekiel 1:4): ״רוח סערה באה מן הצפון ענן גדול ואש מתלקחת ונוגה לו סביב ומתוכה כעין החשמל״ — ofannim, hayyot, the raqi'a above the heads of the hayyot, the likeness of a throne, the likeness as the appearance of a man — behold, here are nine visions, concerning which he said beforehand ״וארא מראות אלהים״, and after the nine they attain the tenth, which is ״כמראה אדם״. But the prophecy of Moses our master, peace be upon him, was through the speculum that illuminates (aspaklaria ha-me'ira). Now go forth and learn what the difference is between the prophecy of Moses and that of all the other prophets, and understand this. And I will further tell you that there are among the sages those who hold that these nine visions are nine visions of the holy living creatures in the nine spheres, for the prophet Ezekiel, peace be upon him, had the heavens opened to him — and they are the nine spheres — and he saw in the ninth sphere, the encompassing one, the four living creatures of the Chariot that are there, near to the Glory; and through their brilliant and mighty radiance, the likeness of the four living creatures appeared in every rakia and rakia as well. And concerning this our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted regarding the creation of the work of Genesis: the lower earth has in it holy living creatures, ofannim, and the Throne of Glory, and it is the footstool of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Isaiah 66:1): ״והארץ הדום רגלי״, and they said this concerning the pavement of the sphere of the moon; and concerning this it is written (Ezekiel 1:15): ״והנה אופן אחד מארץ״, and Targum Jonathan translated מלרע לרום שמיא ('from below to the height of heaven'), for he saw this vision also on the plane of the lunar sphere. And so in every sphere of all the nine there is a likeness of four living creatures. And through the radiance of the four upper living creatures in the upper encompassing sphere — because of the overwhelming intensity of the radiance of the visions in the eight spheres — the prophet had no power to gaze upon and see the likeness of the four living creatures of the Chariot in the ninth encompassing sphere, which are near to the Glory: not in the appearance of a man, nor in the chashmal that surrounds the throne, nor in the cloud that separates between the Glory and himself, for the light of the visions in the eight spheres prevents him from attaining this. And in order to allude to these nine visions in the nine spheres, which the Holy One, blessed be He, established for the glory of Jacob engraved in the Chariot, the phrase עבדי יעקב ('my servant Jacob') is written nine times throughout Scripture, and this matter is among the allusions of the Torah." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'And He called to Moses' — this is what the verse says (Proverbs 25:6–7): ״אל תתהדר לפני מלך ובמקום גדולים אל תעמוד כי טוב אמור לך עלה הנה״ — 'Do not glorify yourself before a king, and do not stand in the place of great men, for it is better that it be said to you: come up here.' So too Moses did not go up until the Holy One, blessed be He, called him, as it says: ויקרא אל משה. This is what the verse says (ibid. v. 29): ״גאות אדם תשפילנו ושפל רוח יתמך כבוד״ — 'A man's pride will bring him low, but one of lowly spirit will be upheld by honor.' Anyone who pursues authority, authority flees from him; and anyone who flees from authority, authority pursues him. Saul fled from authority at the time he was to be made king, as it is said (I Samuel 10:22): ״הנה הוא נחבא אל הכלים״ — he hid himself until they inquired through the Urim and Thummim; authority pursued him, as it is said (ibid.): ״הראיתם אשר בחר בו ה׳״. Abimelech son of Jerubbaal pursued authority and it fled from him, as it is said (Judges 9:1): ״וילך אבימלך בן ירובעל שכמה אל אחי אמו״, (ibid.) ״דברו נא באזני כל בעלי שכם״ etc., (ibid.) ״וישלח אלהים רוח רעה בין אבימלך ובין בעלי שכם״. Moses fled from authority, as it is said (Exodus 4:13): ״לא איש דברים אנכי״ etc., and he said (ibid.): ״שלח נא ביד תשלח״; authority pursued him, as it is said: ויקרא אל משה, and it is written (Psalms 8:6): ״ותחסרהו מעט מאלהים וכבוד והדר תעטרהו״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :register {:flags {2 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {5 "The citation (שמות י״ח:י״ב) for 'משה ידבר והאלהים יעננו בקול' appears to reference Exodus 18:12 per the printed text, but the quoted phrase is not found there; the verse Exodus 18:12 describes Jethro's burnt-offering. Translated as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:23:41.892800867Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}